France, the politics of insecurity increasingly extends beyond questions of crime and public order to encompass deeper struggles over national identity, disappearance of the political centre and erosion of democratic values. The emergence of far-right extremist violence, alongside the persistent stigmatization of ethnically diverse working-class suburbs, raises urgent questions about the relationship between fiery and divisive political rhetoric and far-right radicalization. Downing examines these interrelated challenges against the backdrop of a deadly shooting in the Paris suburbs, allegedly motivated by racist and anti-Muslim hostility.</summary><category term="Spotlight" /></entry><entry><title>'Extremist right-wing ideologies' an increasing threat in France &amp; 'across much of democratic world'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:01d29fd13c7bd66c14585626bca57d01aa35f13d63203d2237d67e4fc7ebba26</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T20:02:03Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-extremist-right-wing-ideologies-an-increasing-threat-in-france-across-much-of-democratic-world" /><summary type="html">François Picard is pleased to welcome Dr Joseph Downing, Senior Lecturer in International Relations and Politics at Aston University. In modern France, the politics of insecurity increasingly extends beyond questions of crime and public order to encompass deeper struggles over national identity, disappearance of the political centre and erosion of democratic values. The emergence of far-right extremist violence, alongside the persistent stigmatization of ethnically diverse working-class suburbs, raises urgent questions about the relationship between political rhetoric, radicalization and the erosion of democratic moderation. Downing examines these interrelated challenges against the backdrop of a deadly shooting in the Paris suburbs, allegedly motivated by racist and anti-Muslim hostility. For Downing, the attack illustrates a broader shift in Europe's extremist landscape, in which the rise of far-right political violence demands greater public and institutional attention: "This is really one of the first and one of the most serious right-wing nationalist, nativist-inspired terror attacks that you see in France."
 
Yet Downing's analysis reaches beyond the horrific attack. He challenges the political representation of France's working-class suburbs as centres of Islamist radicalism, describing such portrayals as a divisive rhetorical strategy that stigmatizes communities whose residents are overwhelmingly engaged in ordinary civic and economic life.
 
His central political concern is the weakening of the democratic centre. Amid rising living costs, social insecurity and the circulation of polarizing identity narratives through digital media, he argues that political movements on the ideological fringes gain an advantage by offering apparently simplistic, and often divisive solutions, to deeply complex problems. "The centre of politics in France, as in many other democracies, is struggling to hold."
 
Perhaps most revealingly, Downing identifies the deliberate or careless conflation of racial identity, migration status and religious extremism as a particularly damaging feature of contemporary political discourse. By treating French citizens of African or Arab heritage as perpetual outsiders, political commentators risk reinforcing the very divisions that undermine national cohesion.
 
The interview therefore opens onto a wider question at the heart of contemporary democratic politics: what happens when narratives of national insecurity become more politically influential than the social realities they claim to describe?
 
Downing's reflections illuminate not only the challenges facing France but also the wider vulnerabilities of democratic societies confronting political polarization, economic anxiety and the normalization of exclusionary rhetoric.</summary></entry><entry><title>Michigan's El-Sayed targeted by Republicans in Midterms race: Will the religion card work?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:18962ff633b2ce2c0cfa3f3b6528c8f6338230ced8f3fed6b5986d646e93b8db</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T19:31:10Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-debate/20261029-michigan-s-el-sayed-targeted-by-republicans-in-midterms-race-will-the-religion-card-work" /><summary type="html">What in the name of political free speech is off-limits? Can you say whatever you want on the campaign trail, no matter how false or racist?</summary><category term="The Debate" /></entry><entry><title>French shooting suspect deliberately targeted Muslims, Africans</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:80db0b7a0f8703498145e322fd73975fe29cabea7527d8885c76349c9ba9ec11</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T18:45:57Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-french-shooting-suspect-deliberately-targeted-muslims-africans" /><summary type="html">A man suspected of a gun attack in the Paris suburbs last week, killing one person and wounding seven including a child, has admitted to "targeting individuals who appeared to be Muslim, African, or North African," prosecutors said on Thursday. The attack came amid an increasingly tense political climate, with just six months to go before next spring's presidential election.</summary></entry><entry><title>'Deeply regressive': How the French government plans to make protesters pay for riots and looting</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:99b65ea63e4ace6e49b74f296f93a1dee6abdccf2793b8c87b49fd3ac867d61d</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T18:15:38Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/france/20261029-deeply-regressive-how-the-french-government-plans-to-make-protesters-pay-for-riots-and-looting" /><summary type="html">As France continues to pick up the pieces after riots and looting broke out on the fringes of nationwide peaceful demonstrations on October 17, the government is pushing forward a bill that would make protesters have to pay up to €10,000 in reparations for damage caused during public unrest – even if they were not directly responsible for the vandalism.</summary><category term="France" /></entry><entry><title>Lt. Gen. Ben Hodges accuses Hegseth of 'getting rid of people who may have the courage to push back'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:46d1cd813a4e58b712e1c533181f50d3e1e704023cc88d3efc73ea52fdd1bbd5</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T18:09:28Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/lt-gen-ben-hodges-accuses-hegseth-of-getting-rid-of-people-who-may-have-the-courage-to-push-back" /><summary type="html">Erin Ogunkeye welcomes Lt. General (ret.) Ben Hodges, former commander of US Army Europe and NATO Allied Land Command. The dismissal of senior military officers without public explanation raises fundamental questions about institutional trust and the relationship between civilian authority and military leadership. For General Hodges, the reported firing of Lt. General Brett Sylvia reflects a potentially troubling pattern within the Pentagon under Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth. Equally striking is his criticism of the reluctance of retired senior commanders to speak publicly against policies they consider damaging. Hodges said he was "disappointed and surprised" by the limited public resistance among former four-star generals.</summary></entry><entry><title>'An absurdity' to blame human error: Spain's deadly train crash 'should never' have happened</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a54648c660b3789af2420a40d47c22fffc80201afd562261a5385103b4a81434</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T17:15:50Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/an-absurdity-to-blame-human-error-spain-s-deadly-train-crash-should-never-have-happened" /><summary type="html">Following Spain's deadly high-speed train crash, Delano D'Souza is joined by Francisco Badea, Director of FEM Expert Engineering, Railway Structural &amp; PRM Certification Expert. Batea challenges explanations that reduce railway disasters to isolated human mistakes. “It is basically an absurdity to say that it depended on one person and one human mistake,” he argues, emphasizing that modern railway networks operate through multiple layers of protection, including automated safeguards, train-detection technologies, operational protocols and institutional oversight. “The system has been designed in a way that this type of accident should never happen.”</summary></entry><entry><title>DR Congo: A centre celebrates the discharge of last Ebola patients</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:bec264a697435e6c7b50e4267e397d66d2ecc11743ce098556c0c44b77052334</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T17:13:52Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-dr-congo-a-centre-celebrates-the-discharge-of-last-ebola-patients" /><summary type="html">Medical staff and residents at a treatment centre in Ituri danced and sang to celebrate a first since May: the centre is no longer treating any confirmed Ebola cases.</summary></entry><entry><title>Ireland becomes refuge for anti-Trump Americans</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:49e899ae33ae1bd25a3c6b90af44b8eb6f8a4cd295fdb0c0e02802e73de3c31e</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T17:08:46Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/reporters/20261029-ireland-becomes-refuge-for-anti-trump-americans" /><summary type="html">They have left everything behind to get away from US President Donald Trump's America. Entrepreneurs, academics, scientists and students: tens of thousands of US citizens have moved to Ireland in recent years. Last year alone, some 10,000 Americans settled there and nearly 100 even applied for asylum – citing political, racial or gender persecution.</summary><category term="Reporters" /></entry><entry><title>Ian McEwan speculates on 'What We Can Know' in the 22nd century</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a299c1a680f1e015eec54467ff3220b4b76bd8fc5480ea92e4c80c7dcb72fbcb</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T16:29:20Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/arts24/20261029-ian-mcewan-speculates-on-what-we-can-know-in-the-22nd-century" /><summary type="html">Booker Prize-winning author Ian McEwan sits down with Olivia Salazar-Winspear to discuss the 22nd century society he created in "What We Can Know", as his latest novel is translated into French. While his characters grapple with the aftermath of the great "derangement", in a world where many of the 21st century's capital cities have been submerged, we discuss our upcoming reckoning with the climate crisis.</summary><category term="arts24" /></entry><entry><title>Flavio Bolsonaro’s great escape: Is this the beginning of the end for ‘Bolsonarism’?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:49fa063d8ee38eed1e310b50c9b4258d1fab260ce39a2e1db9ca7c9df7de158c</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T16:19:17Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/americas/20261029-flavio-bolsonaro-s-great-escape-is-this-the-beginning-of-the-end-for-bolsonarism" /><summary type="html">The Brazilian election results had barely been announced before the defeated presidential candidate, Flavio Bolsonaro, packed his bags and fled with his family to the United States. The move may have dealt a serious blow to the already tarnished family brand, but does it spell the end for “Bolsonarism”?</summary><category term="Americas" /></entry><entry><title>Aid mission set to enter Ukraine's Oleshky as localised ceasefire holds for third day</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:246efb18a72e77f0e8aaa169f160a05f3f1d5a17d220912cfa59cd22d567b437</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T16:19:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/europe/20261029-aid-mission-set-to-enter-ukraine-s-oleshky-as-localised-ceasefire-holds-for-third-day" /><summary type="html">A humanitarian aid mission is set to enter the Ukrainian town of Oleshky, a Ukrainian official said on Thuirsday, as a localised truce between Kyiv and Moscow, brokered by US first lady Melania Trump, held for a third day. Some 2,000 residents in the Russian occupied city have been cut off from essential supplies amid reports that many are starving.</summary><category term="Europe" /></entry><entry><title>A tale of two economies: How America's cost-of-living crisis is shaping US midterm elections</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4a67b638ad16c0c5dadd9e2ec4605de6a4a2497ebd27c460977c16dea6cf8f51</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T16:13:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/people-profit/20261029-a-tale-of-two-economies-how-america-s-cost-of-living-crisis-is-shaping-us-midterm-elections" /><summary type="html">The economy is the number one issue for most American voters, as they worry about high food and energy costs. But can the results of November's midterm elections really impact the cost-of-living crisis? "There isn't a ton that anyone can do about it, regardless of who wins the midterms," says Greg Ip, chief economics commentator at The Wall Street Journal.</summary><category term="People &amp; Profit" /></entry><entry><title>Spain's King Felipe VI and Queen Letizia visit injured in train crash at Toledo hospital</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3fe822d8da55793c78fb0f5148718abaa8e502c8ef8d620d6884db5d85e79d67</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T15:38:49Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-spain-s-king-felipe-vi-and-queen-letizia-visit-injured-in-train-crash-at-toledo-hospital" /><summary type="html">King Felipe VI and Queen Letizia arrived at Toledo University Hospital on Thursday to visit those injured in the train accident in Urda – in which at least 69 people died – who are being treated at this hospital, 15 of whom are in the ICU. The King and Queen will meet with the medical team and subsequently visit several of the injured, who will be accompanied by their families. FRANCE 24's Sarah Morris reports from oledo University Hospital.</summary></entry><entry><title>Ireland opens arms to Americans fleeing US President Donald Trump</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:a1e9f495fa8c9fbdd4ff8825452b898756ac8a033e90c82e57c4a8cccb77ae8e</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T15:31:18Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-ireland-opens-arms-to-americans-fleeing-us-president-donald-trump" /><summary type="html">Tens of thousands of US citizens have fled the United States since Donald Trump's return to power two years ago. Given the language and a shared history, Ireland has been among the top choices for Americans looking for an alternative. FRANCE 24's team reports.</summary></entry><entry><title>Pentagon shake-up continues: US fires top army general in latest ouster</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:303fdc2aa43d781b6cbcdf22d84eb0803cc31f025f8cb13fe36f022fa2ffba5e</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T15:30:44Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-pentagon-shake-up-continues-us-fires-top-army-general-in-latest-ouster" /><summary type="html">The United States has fired its top military planner, The Atlantic reported Wednesday, in a surprise shake-up as President Donald Trump appears to be preparing to resume large-scale strikes against Iran. Army Lieutenant General Brett Sylvia was removed this week from his role as head of strategy, plans and policy on the Joint Chiefs of Staff. His ouster would be the latest in a series of high-profile departures from the military since Trump returned to power a year ago.</summary></entry><entry><title>Food safety in India: A challenge for the world's most populous country</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b693c23f620cf4a5926c5c6a8b5d4edf33ba0de9ebbc702d73e981e4b6500e94</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T15:15:52Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261029-food-safety-in-india-a-challenge-for-the-world-s-most-populous-country--d71fe213e3e3.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/focus/20261029-food-safety-in-india-a-challenge-for-the-world-s-most-populous-country" /><summary type="html">India's food industry feeds more than a billion people every day. But ensuring that what reaches consumers is safe has become an increasingly difficult challenge. The country has witnessed a rise in cases of food adulteration, with sometimes fatal consequences. As concerns over adulteration and poor hygiene grow, questions are being raised about whether regulators can effectively oversee such a sprawling sector.</summary><category term="Focus" /></entry><entry><title>US Midterms: Donald Trump and Barack Obama make closing pitches</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9918d65948ff566b543ab19d4cef97109e709a828d49f9aa44399149845daa23</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T14:57:48Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-us-midterms-donald-trump-and-barack-obama-make-closing-pitches--51abaa1b21c1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-us-midterms-donald-trump-and-barack-obama-make-closing-pitches" /><summary type="html">In the United States, Democrats are turning to Barack Obama to rally voters ahead of the midterm elections, a crucial test that could shape the remainder of Donald Trump's presidency. The former president has addressed three campaign rallies this week, with the latest one in Atlanta, Georgia. FRANCE 24's International Affairs Editor Angela Diffley has more.</summary></entry><entry><title>Spain reckons with second deadly train crash of the year ahead of national election</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4939fcf894efbdcf1f38acf553c21dc40d541839dfa69b0dc2587648ab6d0f32</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T14:50:53Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-spain-reckons-with-second-deadly-train-crash-of-the-year-ahead-of-national-election--79ed68a714bf.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-spain-reckons-with-second-deadly-train-crash-of-the-year-ahead-of-national-election" /><summary type="html">Wednesday's deadly train crash has dealt a serious blow to Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez’s campaign. The accident in Toledo province killed 69 people and came just nine months after another deadly crash on the same line, leading to accusations from experts and the public of underinvestment in railway infrastructure under Sanchez's government after years of rapid network ​expansion.</summary></entry><entry><title>Trump and Obama trade barbs in closing week of US midterm election</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:2823b8657a2073759236d83197c97a6396ad0764ff906102f0eb9535c483b17d</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T14:48:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-trump-and-obama-trade-barbs-in-closing-week-of-us-midterm-election--eced57ce1300.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-trump-and-obama-trade-barbs-in-closing-week-of-us-midterm-election" /><summary type="html">Neither man is on the ​ballot but Republican President Donald Trump and Democratic former President Barack Obama took prominent roles on the campaign trail on Wednesday, with less than a week to go before congressional races that could shift the balance of power in Washington. Thirty-five ‌of the 100 Senate seats and all 435 seats in the House of Representatives are being contested next Tuesday. Nonpartisan analysts generally favor Democrats' chances of capturing a majority in the ⁠House, with the contest for the Senate seen as very close.</summary></entry><entry><title>Minimum wage increase could come early as France braces for protests</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:3f6ec49e75f717392ad44fd66169673a31dab314cf4c39328bcc6cc8d8dc5c1e</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T14:47:48Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-minimum-wage-increase-could-come-early-as-france-braces-for-protests--72eb1a78a4e1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-minimum-wage-increase-could-come-early-as-france-braces-for-protests" /><summary type="html">The French government is looking for new ways to calm social unrest ahead of more planned demonstrations over the cost of living this weekend. The minimum wage was set to increase on January 1, 2027. Now, officials are considering making the change one month earlier and adding an extra boost.</summary></entry><entry><title>'Real questions to be asked' after deadly train collision in Spain, journalist says</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8627435cf457edb6b8c2c12cfd86b8c5b7c044a997562c2d2936a37ec74a5b16</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T14:44:07Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261029-real-questions-to-be-asked-after-deadly-train-collision-in-spain-journalist-says--1490159b0812.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/perspective/20261029-real-questions-to-be-asked-after-deadly-train-collision-in-spain-journalist-says" /><summary type="html">In this edition of Perspective, we focus on the tragic train collision that killed at least 68 people in Spain on Wednesday. We're joined by Nicholas Kingsley, executive editor of Railway Gazette International.</summary><category term="Perspective" /></entry><entry><title>Why is Donald Trump avoiding the Epstein case at all costs?</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f723057ffd80fb6f99cd9719ed8dad87aba9183c8c89c91f33c5025c76e78d4a</id><updated>2026-10-29T21:14:43Z</updated><published>2026-10-29T14:37:59Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-why-is-donald-trump-avoiding-the-epstein-case-at-all-costs--926236f51e51.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20261029-why-is-donald-trump-avoiding-the-epstein-case-at-all-costs" /><summary type="html">The midterm elections campaign is in full swing in the United States, but Donald Trump is doing his utmost to avoid one subject: the Epstein Files. On January 30, the US Department of Justice published the court records relating to the Epstein case. At FRANCE 24, two of our journalists, Samia Metheni and Ava Luquet, delve into the 3 million documents to understand how Epstein's network operated. What did they find looking through the documents and what impact do they still have on Trump? Ava Luquet answered François Picard's questions.</summary></entry></feed>
